Third-party software
--------------------

npm packages shipped by the server and web app, with their licenses, are listed
in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md (generated by `npm run licenses`). Notable ones:

- audiomotion-analyzer (the visualizer): AGPL-3.0-or-later,
  https://github.com/hvianna/audioMotion-analyzer
- sharp and libvips (artwork): Apache-2.0 and LGPL-3.0-or-later,
  https://github.com/lovell/sharp

The Docker image adds, from Debian bookworm (source at https://sources.debian.org):

- FFmpeg (transcoding): LGPL-2.1-or-later, with GPL-2.0-or-later parts as built
  by Debian, https://ffmpeg.org
- Chromaprint / fpcalc (audio fingerprints): LGPL-2.1-or-later,
  https://acoustid.org/chromaprint
- Node.js: MIT, https://nodejs.org

The "gpu" image adds the lyric aligner's Python packages (aligner/requirements.txt):

- PyTorch and torchaudio: BSD-3-Clause, https://pytorch.org; the CUDA wheels
  include NVIDIA CUDA runtime libraries under NVIDIA's license terms
  (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/eula/)
- openai-whisper: MIT, https://github.com/openai/whisper (model weights are
  downloaded on first use, MIT)
- stable-ts: MIT, https://github.com/jianfch/stable-ts
- demucs: MIT, https://github.com/adefossez/demucs
- NumPy, Numba, llvmlite: BSD-style; tiktoken: MIT

The desktop app is built with Electron (MIT) and electron-builder (MIT), and uses
bonjour-service (MIT) and castv2-client (MIT). The phone app is built with Expo
and React Native (MIT) and react-native-webview (MIT).

Data
----

Lyrics, artwork and metadata are fetched at run time from public services and
stored on your server; none ship with Slopify. MusicBrainz and ListenBrainz data
are CC0. Respect each service's terms and the rights of artists in what you
download and share. See PRIVACY.md for every service Slopify contacts.
